/**

@page page_exahype2_tutorials_elastic Part 5 - 3D Elastic Waves and a Taste of HPC

\tableofcontents

The final tutorial steps up to **three dimensions** and to a system with **nine
unknowns**: the linear elastic wave equations, the workhorse of computational
seismology. An isotropic *explosion* at the centre of a cube radiates an outgoing
spherical wave. We visualise it by **slicing the 3D cube** interactively. A 3D
high-order ADER-DG solver is also a genuinely **HPC-scale** workload. This part
doubles as a tour of how ExaHyPE uses parallel hardware. We measure its OpenMP
strong scaling directly. The notebook is
`tutorials/05_elastic/ElasticWaves.ipynb` with its implementation in
`ADERDGSolver.cpp`.

# The Elastic Wave Equations

We use the first-order **velocity-stress** form. The unknowns are the velocity
\f$\mathbf{v} = (v_x, v_y, v_z)\f$ and the symmetric stress tensor
\f$\boldsymbol{\sigma} = (\sigma_{xx}, \sigma_{yy}, \sigma_{zz}, \sigma_{xy},
\sigma_{xz}, \sigma_{yz})\f$ - nine fields in total:

@f$
\begin{equation*}
    \rho\, \partial_t v_i = \partial_j \sigma_{ij}, \qquad
    \partial_t \sigma_{ij} = \lambda\, \delta_{ij}\, \partial_k v_k
    + \mu \left( \partial_i v_j + \partial_j v_i \right).
\end{equation*}
@f$

Here \f$\rho\f$ is the density and \f$\lambda, \mu\f$ the Lamé parameters. The
medium supports two wave types: fast **P-waves** at
\f$c_p = \sqrt{(\lambda + 2\mu)/\rho}\f$ and slower **S-waves** at
\f$c_s = \sqrt{\mu/\rho}\f$. We pick \f$\rho = 1\f$, \f$c_p = 2\f$, \f$c_s = 1\f$.
The isotropic explosion (a Gaussian bump on the diagonal stresses) excites only
P-waves. We expect a clean expanding spherical shell.

# The Configuration

A \f$3 \times 3 \times 3\f$ cube centred on the origin with non-periodic walls. The
only change from the 2D tutorials is `dimensions = 3` and the three-component
`size`/`offset`/`periodic_bc`. The solver is an ADER-DG scheme of order 4 whose
unknowns are a velocity of multiplicity 3 and a symmetric \f$3 \times 3\f$ stress
tensor:

    my_project = exahype2.Project(
        namespace    = ["tutorials", "exahype2", "elastic"],
        project_name = "Elastic",
        executable   = "Elastic",
        dimensions   = 3,
        size         = [ 3.0,  3.0,  3.0],
        offset       = [-1.5, -1.5, -1.5],
        periodic_bc  = [False, False, False],
    )

    aderdg_solver = exahype2.dg.ader.Solver(
        name                 = "ADERDGSolver",
        order                = 4,
        unknowns             = {"v": 3, "sigma": exahype2.SymmetricMatrix(3)},
        auxiliary_variables  = {},
        time_stepping        = exahype2.TimeStepping.Adaptive(0.9),
        linear               = True,
    )
    aderdg_solver.set_pde_terms(
        initial_condition   = True,
        boundary_conditions = True,
        flux                = True,
        max_eigenvalue      = True,
    )
    my_project.add_solver(aderdg_solver)

# The Implementation File

`ADERDGSolver.cpp` contains the PDE terms. The code addresses the unknowns by their names
(`Q.v(axis)`, `Q.sigma(row, column)`).
`initialCondition` sets the Gaussian explosion (equal diagonal stresses, at rest);
`boundaryConditions` gives a quiescent exterior to reflect off; `maxEigenvalue`
returns the P-wave speed \f$c_p\f$; and `flux` is the velocity-stress flux in the
direction `normal`, with the Lamé parameters recovered from \f$\rho, c_p, c_s\f$.

@include tutorials/05_elastic/ADERDGSolver.cpp

# Running and Visualising in 3D

A 3D high-order run is heavier than the 2D tutorials - at order 4 each cell stores
\f$(4+1)^3 = 125\f$ degrees of freedom per unknown:

    export OMP_NUM_THREADS=4
    ./Elastic -et 0.5 -ns 10 -l 2

A 3D field is best explored slice by slice. `show_solution_3D` cuts axis-aligned
2D slices through the cube: pick the slice axis with the **slice ⊥** dropdown
(x/y/z), move the **slice** slider through the volume, pick a field, and animate
over time with the **time** slider / Play. Picking a slice (or the field or axis)
renders all of that slice's time frames in parallel across cores during a brief
*Prefetching N/M*. The time slider and playback then run smoothly:

    from postprocessing import render_solution
    render_solution.show_solution_3D("ADERDGSolver", field="sigma")

The expanding spherical P-wave appears in the mid-plane slice as concentric
rings - the cross-section of the spherical shell. Moving the slice slider
towards the cube's faces makes the rings shrink, exactly as cutting a sphere
off-centre should:

@image html documentation/exahype2/tutorials/Elastic_slice.png

@note The pictures in this part were produced at `-l 3`, a grid of \f$k^3\f$
cells per axis, with k the subdivision factor of the active curve (3 on the
Peano curve, 2 on the Hilbert curve). That run takes a few minutes. The default
level 2 shows the same physics, only coarser - raise the level in the run
command if you want the same crispness.

# A Taste of HPC

A 3D high-order ADER-DG solver is exactly the kind of compute-bound workload HPC
exists for. ExaHyPE exposes parallelism through **two independent control
surfaces**, neither of which needs a code change:

- **Distributed memory (MPI).** Launch under `mpirun -n N ./Elastic ...`: ExaHyPE
  decomposes the domain across the ranks and balances the load automatically. MPI
  is built in by default; the banner reports `MPI   N Ranks on M Nodes` plus the
  `Load Balance` of the `MPI Decomposition` statistics.
- **Threads (OpenMP).** The thread count per rank is the `OMP_NUM_THREADS`
  environment variable; the startup banner echoes it as `OpenMP   N Threads`.

The notebook measures the second one, **OpenMP strong scaling**: it runs the same
problem on 1, 2 and 4 threads of one process, reads the `Average Time Step` the
solver prints (with plotting off, `-ns 0`, to time pure compute), and plots the
speedup against the ideal linear line. On one node, threads scale better than as
many MPI ranks with one thread each. Every rank copies the faces on the boundary
of its subdomain into messages and waits in MPI for the faces of its neighbours
in every time step. The threads of one process read these faces in place.

# Exercise - Launch a Shear Wave

The isotropic explosion sets the three **diagonal** stresses equal. That is a pure
volume change. It radiates only the fast **P-wave**, the clean spherical shell (a
ring in any slice). A **shear** source, a single *off-diagonal* stress, launches
the slower **S-wave** (\f$c_s = 1\f$, half the P-wave speed). Its radiation forms
a four-lobed **quadrupole**.

In `ADERDGSolver.cpp`, inside `initialCondition`, replace the three diagonal-stress
lines

    Q.sigma(0, 0) = bump; // sigma_xx
    Q.sigma(1, 1) = bump; // sigma_yy
    Q.sigma(2, 2) = bump; // sigma_zz

with a single off-diagonal (shear) component:

    Q.sigma(0, 1) = bump; // sigma_xy (shear) -> launches an S-wave

Rebuild and re-run, then view the velocity field sliced through the cube's
mid-plane (slice axis `z`, middle slice position). In place of the P-wave's ring
you will see the four lobes of the S-wave:

@image html documentation/exahype2/tutorials/Elastic_shear.png

# Experiment

- Raise `level` to 3 for a much heavier run and re-measure the scaling - bigger
  problems usually scale better.
- Go hybrid: give each rank several threads, e.g.
  `OMP_NUM_THREADS=4 mpirun -n 2 ./Elastic -et 0.3 -ns 0 -l 2`, and watch both
  banner lines. On clusters this is the standard configuration - one rank per
  node or socket, threads inside.
- Raise the `order` (e.g. 5) for more accuracy and far more arithmetic per cell.

That concludes the tutorial series - you have driven ADER-DG and Finite Volumes,
static mesh refinement, a-posteriori limiting, and a parallel 3D
solver. Congratulations!

*/
